An extensible Model Context Protocol server for Isabelle
=========================================================

`isabelle mcp_server` lets an AI agent drive the Isabelle proof
assistant over the Model Context Protocol (MCP): step through Isar
proofs in server-side repls, run sledgehammer and find_theorems, load
and verify theory files from disk, and explore the session library —
all against one long-running prover session, so the agent never pays
Isabelle's startup cost twice.

The server is also extensible from inside the logic: any theory can
register new MCP tools and resources with the `mcp_tool` /
`mcp_resource` commands (see "Extending the server" below), and they
appear to the agent next to the builtins.

Requirements
------------

- Isabelle2025-2. This repository bundles a Linux distribution at
  `Isabelle2025-2_linux/Isabelle2025-2/bin/isabelle`; a standard
  install works the same. Below, `isabelle` means whichever executable
  you use — give your MCP client the absolute path if it is not on
  PATH.
- Python 3 (only for the optional smoke test).

Installation
------------

1. Clone this repository.

2. Register the `mcp/` component in user-space (once per user/machine):

       isabelle components -u /path/to/isabelle-mcp/mcp

3. Pre-build the proving session so the first agent connection does
   not wait for a full HOL build (the server builds missing heaps
   itself, but HOL from scratch takes a while):

       isabelle build -b -d /path/to/isabelle-mcp/mcp/Tools MCP-HOL

4. (Optional) smoke-test the installation end to end:

       python3 mcp/test/test_mcp.py

   and, if you also registered the `mcp_test/` component
   (`isabelle components -u /path/to/isabelle-mcp/mcp_test`), run the
   unit suites with `isabelle mcp_test` (add `-b` for the slower
   bridge tests against a live session).

Running the server
------------------

    isabelle mcp_server -s MCP-HOL

speaks newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0 (MCP) on stdin/stdout and logs
progress to stderr; it exits when stdin closes. Your MCP client
launches and owns this process — you rarely run it by hand.

Options:

    -d DIR       include a session directory (repeatable; default:
                 $ISABELLE_MCP_HOME/Tools)
    -s SESSION   session to run (default: MCP-Tools, the minimal
                 Pure-based registry — for actual proving you want
                 MCP-HOL, which is HOL plus the repl/search tools)
    -T THEORY    theory registering the MCP tools (default: MCP_Tools)

Verifying your own theories
---------------------------

The agent verifies local theories with the `load_theory` /
`check_theory` tools; diagnostics, source and entities are served as
`isabelle://theory/{name}` resources. Two setups, by how your
theories are organized:

**Plain directory of .thy files** (imports available in the running
image, e.g. `Main`): no configuration needed. The agent calls

    load_theory {"name": "MyTheory", "master_dir": "/path/to/dir"}

which loads and checks the theory with its transitive dependencies
and reports per-theory ok/error status with positions. After edits,
`check_theory` re-verifies.

**A project with a ROOT file** (its own session): start the server
with your project directory added,

    isabelle mcp_server -s MCP-HOL -d /path/to/isabelle-mcp/mcp/Tools -d /path/to/your/project

(note: passing any `-d` replaces the default, so keep the
`mcp/Tools` entry). Your session then appears in the discovery tools
— `list_sessions`, `list_theories`, `search_sources` — and theories
load by session-qualified name: `load_theory {"name":
"MySession.MyTheory"}`.

Beyond batch verification, the repl tools (`repl_init`, `repl_step`,
`repl_state`, ...) let the agent develop a proof interactively, with
`sledgehammer` and `find_theorems` available at every step.

Configuring the agent
---------------------

Any MCP client that supports stdio servers works. For Claude Code,
from your project directory:

    claude mcp add isabelle -- isabelle mcp_server -s MCP-HOL -d /path/to/isabelle-mcp/mcp/Tools -d .

or equivalently in `.mcp.json`:

    {
      "mcpServers": {
        "isabelle": {
          "command": "isabelle",
          "args": ["mcp_server", "-s", "MCP-HOL",
                   "-d", "/path/to/isabelle-mcp/mcp/Tools",
                   "-d", "."]
        }
      }
    }

Use an absolute path for `"command"` if `isabelle` is not on the
client's PATH. The first connection blocks until the session heap is
loaded (seconds when pre-built, see Installation step 3); clients
with short startup timeouts may need that pre-build.

Extending the server
--------------------

MCP tools are Isabelle context entities: a theory registers them, and
they are visible to the agent when the running session imports that
theory. A minimal registration theory:

    theory My_Tools
      imports "MCP-Tools.MCP_Tools"
    begin

    (* wrap a diagnostic Isar command as a tool; the description is
       mandatory and the command name is quoted *)
    mcp_tool "find_consts" (description \<open>Search for constants by
      name pattern or type.\<close>)

    (* expose a named fact collection as a read-time resource *)
    mcp_resource simps

    end

Add the theory to a session of your own (based on `MCP-HOL`) and run
the server with `-s YourSession`. Tools registered mid-session emit
`tools/list_changed` notifications, so the agent picks them up live.
There are further forms — string functions, ML run functions with
typed parameters — documented in `mcp/Tools/MCP_Tools.thy`.

Project layout
--------------

    mcp/        the server component (Scala + the MCP-Tools/MCP-HOL sessions)
    mcp_test/   munit test suites (`isabelle mcp_test`)
    ir/         the I/R proving engine (reused verbatim by MCP-HOL)
    spec        the full design record; CHANGELOG tracks decisions
    plans/      per-tool implementation plans; evals/ agent evaluations
